// The explicit trust boundary.
//
// Every member is a compiler built-in, each one a promise the compiler cannot
// check: `raw_data`, `string_view` and `cstring_view` discard or manufacture
// provenance, `transmute` reinterprets bits, `forget` suppresses cleanup, `new`
// and `try_new` allocate storage nothing owns, `free` releases storage nothing
// traced, and `write` and `take` move a value into and out of uninitialized
// capacity. Importing this package is also what permits the unchecked pointer
// conversions and C-pointer indexing and slicing in the importing file.
package unsafe;

// The address of the first element of `data`, as a C pointer that carries
// neither a length nor a read-only capability, and whose lifetime is no longer
// checked. `data` is a slice, a mutable slice, a dynamic array, a pointer to a
// fixed array, or a `string`:
//
//     raw_data([]$E)          -> [^]E   // read-only slices; the capability is discarded
//     raw_data([]mut $E)      -> [^]E
//     raw_data([dynamic]$E)   -> [^]E
//     raw_data(^[$N]$E)       -> [^]E   // one array level at a time
//     raw_data(string)        -> [^]byte
@(intrinsic)
raw_data :: proc(data: []$E) -> [^]E ---;

// The `length` bytes at `pointer` as a `string_view`, or `.none` when they are
// not UTF-8. The caller promises the bytes outlive the view; a negative
// `length` panics, as `pointer[0:length]` does.
@(intrinsic)
string_view :: proc(pointer: [^]u8, length: int) -> Option(string_view) ---;

// The NUL-terminated bytes at `pointer` as a `cstring_view`. The caller
// promises they outlive the view; a null `pointer` panics.
@(intrinsic)
cstring_view :: proc(pointer: [^]u8) -> cstring_view ---;

// Consumes an owning value and runs no cleanup hook, for it or for anything it
// owns transitively: the resource is deliberately leaked, or relinquished to
// whatever owns it now. It is not a lifetime extension: a borrow of the
// operand dies here exactly as it would at a `drop`, so a place is written
// `forget(move(place))` and a temporary is passed directly.
@(intrinsic)
forget :: proc(value: move $T) ---;

// Releases an allocation the compiler cannot follow back to the `new` that
// made it: one reached through a `rawptr`, a parameter, or foreign code. It
// checks nothing. That the pointer is that allocation's base, that the
// allocator is the one that created it, that nothing still refers to it, and
// that this happens once are all the caller's.
//
// The operand is a typed `[^]T` or `^mut T`, because the release is *sized*: a
// pointer that travelled as a `rawptr` is cast back to the pointee type at the
// call, and that cast is what supplies the size and alignment to release. A
// handle over an opaque control block, `Shared(T)` among them, has no other
// way to release it. The value is not dropped.
@(intrinsic)
free :: proc(pointer: [^]$T, allocator: Allocator = {}) ---;

// Allocates storage for one `T`, takes `value` into it as `box` does, and
// returns its address. Nothing owns the allocation: the value is never dropped
// and the storage never released unless the program does it, and the address
// carries no provenance.
@(intrinsic)
new :: proc(value: $T, allocator: Allocator = {}) -> [^]T ---;

// `new`, returning an allocation failure instead of applying the allocator's
// policy to it.
@(intrinsic)
try_new :: proc(value: $T, allocator: Allocator = {}) -> Result([^]T, Allocator_Error) ---;

// Reads the value out of `place`, a field or element holding one, and runs no
// cleanup for what is left behind; the storage then holds the bits of a value
// that no longer exists. A bare variable is refused, since `move` already
// takes one's value out.
@(intrinsic)
take :: proc(place: $T) -> T ---;

// Stores `value` into `place`, a field or element holding no value, so nothing
// is dropped first. The value arrives as an initialization takes it: a
// borrowed place is cloned, a temporary or a `move` hands ownership over. A
// bare variable is refused, since an assignment already puts one back.
@(intrinsic)
write :: proc(place: $T, value: T) ---;

// Reads the storage of `value` as a `T`, with no addressable operand: the
// pointer cast `(^T)(&value)^` written as one expression. The compiler checks
// the shape of the conversion and nothing else: the source and destination are
// the same size, and both have a trivial lifecycle. A managed value, a `^T`, a
// slice, a view, or a `dyn` is rejected on either side, as is an aggregate
// containing one: a bit cast may neither duplicate an owning representation
// nor hand back a borrow nothing loaned.
//
// Everything else is the caller's obligation. Producing a valid destination
// representation is not checked at runtime; a *constant* pattern that is not
// a value of the destination type at all, such as a `bool` outside `{0, 1}`,
// an enum with no such member, or a `rune` outside the scalar range, is a
// compile error instead, since there is no caller left to hold responsible. A
// constant operand folds, which is how a package builds an infinity or a NaN:
// neither has a literal spelling. At compile time the storage is read byte by
// byte, so arrays and structs fold as well, except where the result would read
// padding. A union and a record with uninitialized capacity have no
// compile-time meaning.
//
// Pointer destinations are `rawptr` and C pointers only, and the result is
// unchecked: dereferencing it is valid only when the bits already describe
// suitably aligned, live storage of the destination pointee type.
@(intrinsic)
transmute :: proc($T: type, value: $S) -> T ---;
